# Every numeric limit in the workspace, in one file. Read by core/hooks/file_law.py
# (Python) and sourced directly by check-line-counts.sh (shell) — same file, one law.
#
# Two levels each, deliberately symmetric: a WARN asks for a look, a BLOCK stops the commit.
# Size is a signal, never a quota to game — see core/SCHEMA.md § Routing depth and locality.

# Lines in one code file.
WARN_LINES=150
BLOCK_LINES=200

# Code files in one directory. Splitting an over-full directory costs one routing hop, so
# the WARN is where a split becomes worth considering and the BLOCK is where the directory
# holds more than one responsibility no matter how you read it. A directory at 8-9 files is
# a warning on purpose: a split saving two table rows does not pay for its hop.
WARN_FILES=7
BLOCK_FILES=10

# Tokens in a CONTEXT.md *head* — the curated prose above the generated routing block.
# CONTEXT.md is the only enforced-read type, so a token here is paid by every session that
# touches the subtree, while a sibling SPECS.md is paid only on demand. This is the line
# above which a head is asked to justify itself, not a cap: see core/SCHEMA.md § Placement.
# WARN only, deliberately — thinness is a judgement about what the prose *is*, and a block
# would just push constraint prose into a second file without answering that question.
CONTEXT_HEAD_WARN=400

# Tokens of session context. Not a cap — the context window is far larger. These are the two
# ends of the measured cost climb, re-measured 2026-08-13 over 119 sessions / 18,243 turns
# (reproduce with core/tools/wos/session/usage):
#
#     0k- 50k  $0.087/turn    —      200k-250k  $0.202/turn   +5%   <- plateau
#    50k-100k  $0.102/turn  +17%     250k-300k  $0.218/turn   +8%
#   100k-150k  $0.147/turn  +45%  <- bend       300k-400k  $0.269/turn  +24%
#   150k-200k  $0.193/turn  +31%          400k+ $0.373/turn  +39%
#
# Cost does not climb smoothly: it is flat below 100k, climbs hard to 200k, then sits on a
# plateau at ~2x the cheap rate and never comes back down. So WARN marks where the climb
# starts and LOUD where it finishes. Moved in from 150k/250k, which fired halfway up.
#
# Firing these earlier costs almost no extra noise because sessions are bimodal — median peak
# context 59k, p75 271k, little in between — so WARN fires in 48% of sessions instead of 44%.
# And there is runway to act: at CTX_WARN the median session still has ~211 turns ahead, so a
# hand-off has time to pay back its re-grounding cost.
#
# Read by core/hooks/session/context-meter.py, which announces each threshold once per session
# and never blocks.
CTX_WARN=100000
CTX_LOUD=200000
